  "userdoc": {
    "kind": "user",
    "methods": {
      "RECORDS_KIND()": {
        "notice": "The records module kind, in the one pattern every kind follows:         `s2s.module.records`. Must equal the implementation's         `moduleKind()`, and it is the kind this factory approves for a         module upgrade."
      },
      "createDraftsModule(address)": {
        "notice": "Deploy a drafts proxy for `safe`. Permissionless on purpose: see         the contract docblock — a proxy is not an installation."
      },
      "createOwnerHistoryModule(address)": {
        "notice": "Deploy an owner-history proxy for `safe` and the guard bound to         it. Permissionless on purpose, same as `createDraftsModule`: the         guard is only live once the Safe calls `setGuard(guard)`, which         installs nothing but the guard slot."
      },
      "createRecordsModule(address)": {
        "notice": "Deploy the records module for `safe`, or return the module already         recorded for it. Permissionless like the other two creates: a         proxy is not an installation."
      },
      "deployer()": {
        "notice": "The account that deployed the factory; the only address allowed         to move any implementation pointer."
      },
      "draftsImplementation()": {
        "notice": "The implementation the next `createDraftsModule` proxies onto."
      },
      "draftsModuleAddress(address)": {
        "notice": "The address the next `createDraftsModule(safe)` would deploy to,         for the current implementation and the current nonce. See the         three facts above."
      },
      "getDraftsImplementationByVersion(uint256)": {
        "notice": "1-based; reverts `InvalidVersion` outside `1..count`."
      },
      "getOwnerHistoryImplementationByVersion(uint256)": {
        "notice": "1-based; reverts `InvalidVersion` outside `1..count`."
      },
      "getRecordsImplementationByVersion(uint256)": {
        "notice": "1-based; reverts `InvalidVersion` outside `1..count`."
      },
      "guardFor(address)": {
        "notice": "The guard deployed for `module`, or `address(0)` when this         factory deployed none. Answers zero rather than reverting: an         unknown module is a caller's mistake, not a reason a reader dies."
      },
      "isApproved(bytes32,address)": {
        "notice": "Whether `implementation` is this kind's current implementation."
      },
      "latestImplementation(bytes32)": {
        "notice": "The records implementation for `kind`, or zero for any other kind."
      },
      "ownerHistoryImplementation()": {
        "notice": "The implementation the next `createOwnerHistoryModule` proxies onto."
      },
      "ownerHistoryModuleAddress(address)": {
        "notice": "The address the next `createOwnerHistoryModule(safe)` would deploy         its proxy to. The guard is a plain CREATE with no predicted         address, and it is deployed after the proxy, so it cannot move         the proxy. See the three facts above."
      },
      "protocolSafe()": {
        "notice": "The protocol Safe. The records implementation's constructor         reads it back through `protocolSafe()` and compares it with its         second argument; this factory does not treat it as an authority."
      },
      "recordsImplementation()": {
        "notice": "The implementation the next `createRecordsModule` proxies onto."
      },
      "recordsModuleAddress(address)": {
        "notice": "The recorded records module for `safe`, else the address         `createRecordsModule(safe)` would deploy at right now, else         `address(0)` when no implementation is set. See the three facts         above."
      },
      "setRecordsImplementation(address)": {
        "notice": "Set the records implementation and approve it as this module         kind's upgrade target."
      }
    },
    "notice": "The author side's one deploy path: a UUPS proxy per author Safe for         the drafts log, one for the owner history plus the         `OwnerHistoryGuard` wired to that proxy, and one for the records         module.         One factory rather than three, because the modules are installed         together on the same Safe and a reader that has this address has         every author proxy it can verify against. The implementations keep         separate version histories — moving one must never silently repoint         another. Trust model, stated because both halves are easy to get backwards:   - `setDraftsImplementation` / `setOwnerHistoryImplementation` /     `setRecordsImplementation` are `onlyDeployer`. Without that gate anyone     could repoint the `*Implementation()` pointer and the next     `create*Module` would hand an author a proxy that, once installed,     executes as the Safe. The deployer is an immutable set at construction,     so there is no admin to rotate and none to compromise.   - all three `create*Module` stay open **deliberately**: deploying a proxy     costs the caller gas and installs nothing on any Safe. Nothing this     contract does reaches the Safe's storage — no `enableModule`, no     `setGuard` — so a proxy (and, for the history, a guard) is just two     addresses until the Safe adopts them in its own transaction. Gating     creation would buy nothing and would put a trusted party between an     author and their own module.   - `draftsModules` / `ownerHistoryModules` / `guards` are **indexes, not     proof**. `create*Module` is permissionless, so anyone can append an entry     for any Safe, and `guardFor` records what *this* factory made — it does     not prove the Safe installed it. Intersect the Safe's one real adoption     record (the guard slot) before treating an entry as installed.   - `recordsModule` is different in one way: it holds one module per Safe,     written once and never appended to. It is still not proof of     installation — a proxy exists before any Safe enables it — but it is the     module `createRecordsModule` will return on every later call, so a     reader can rely on the address not moving.   - `protocolSafe` is not an authority here. It is the address the records     implementation compares its own constructor argument against, so it     answers that sanity check and nothing else. The gate that decides which     implementations this factory deploys and approves is `deployer`.",
    "version": 1
  }
